{%- comment -%}
  Returns fine print, self-enabling.

  Renders ONLY when a refund policy actually exists in Settings → Policies. Until
  then it outputs nothing, so the storefront can never reference a returns term
  the business has not published. The moment the policy goes live this appears on
  its own, with no theme edit.

  COMPLIANCE — why this sits on the product page and not only in the footer:
  material terms must be *clear and conspicuous*, which means visible at the point
  of decision. A restriction a buyer only discovers after purchase is a deceptive
  omission, and it is also what turns a refund request into a chargeback. Stating
  it plainly next to the buy button protects the term rather than weakening it: a
  disclosed restriction is enforceable, a hidden one is not.

  IMPORTANT: the summary text is editable, and it must match whatever refund
  policy you actually published. The default deliberately makes no specific
  promise beyond linking to the policy — if your policy excludes worn items, say
  so here, because that is the term buyers most need to see before paying.

  Accepts:
  - context: {String} 'product' | 'cart'
  - text: {String} optional summary line; falls back to a neutral default
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if shop.refund_policy != blank and shop.refund_policy.body != blank -%}
  <div class="sw-returns-note sw-returns-note--{{ context | default: 'product' }}">
    <p class="sw-returns-note__line">
      {%- render 'stridewell-icon', icon: 'return-arrow', class: 'sw-icon--sm' -%}
      <span>
        {%- if text != blank -%}
          {{ text }}
        {%- else -%}
          Returns are accepted on unused items in their original packaging.
        {%- endif -%}
        See our <a href="{{ shop.refund_policy.url }}">{{ shop.refund_policy.title }}</a>
        for the full terms.
      </span>
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
